@*
    The reference fragment (task 3.1). Every later list view copies this shape.

    Four rules it exists to demonstrate, all of which the Phase 3 exit criterion checks:

      1. NO `style=` ATTRIBUTE, EVER. All styling is a class in wwwroot/css/dndump.css. A resync
         that changes a colour or a spacing edits that stylesheet's tokens and nothing here.
      2. The htmx contract is markup (SERVER.md §5.1): #v-{view} is the fragment's stable id and
         swaps outerHTML, #rows-{view} is the infinite-scroll append target. Both are derived from
         Model.View.Name so they cannot drift from the route.
      3. No conditionals beyond presence/absence, and no formatting logic. Anything cleverer than
         "render this value" belongs in DotNetDump.Web.Rendering.Display or the view model --
         a template a designer cannot safely re-generate is a template that has failed.
      4. The type column truncates in the middle, never at the end. See MiddleTruncated: the tail
         of a .NET type name is what distinguishes it, so cutting the tail loses the answer.
*@
@model ListModel<HeapStatItem>

<div id="v-@Model.View.Name">
    <table class="dn-table">
        <thead>
            <tr>
                <th scope="col" class="dn-th dn-th--mono">MethodTable</th>
                <th scope="col" class="dn-th dn-th--num">Count</th>
                <th scope="col" class="dn-th dn-th--num">Total size</th>
                <th scope="col" class="dn-th">Type</th>
            </tr>
        </thead>
        <tbody id="rows-@Model.View.Name">
            @foreach (var item in Model.Result.Items)
            {
                var type = MiddleTruncated.From(item.TypeName);
                <tr class="dn-tr">
                    <td class="dn-td dn-td--mono"><span class="dn-addr">@Display.Address(item.MethodTable)</span></td>
                    <td class="dn-td dn-td--num">@Display.Count(item.Count)</td>
                    <td class="dn-td dn-td--num" title="@Display.Bytes(item.TotalSize)">@Display.Size(item.TotalSize)</td>
                    <td class="dn-td dn-td--type" title="@type.Full">
                        <span class="dn-trunc"><span class="dn-trunc__head">@type.Head</span><span class="dn-trunc__tail">@type.Tail</span></span>
                    </td>
                </tr>
            }

            @if (Model.Result.Items.Count == 0)
            {
                <tr>
                    <td colspan="4" class="dn-empty">
                        @* Three different empty states are distinguished, per DESIGN_BRIEF.md §5:
                           an unfiltered empty result means the dump holds nothing of this kind,
                           whereas a filtered one means the filter is too narrow -- and telling a
                           user "no data" when they have simply over-filtered sends them looking
                           for a problem in the dump. *@
                        @if (Model.Result.TotalUnfiltered > 0)
                        {
                            <div class="dn-empty__title">No rows match the current filter.</div>
                            <a class="dn-btn" href="/views/@Model.View.Name">Clear filters</a>
                        }
                        else
                        {
                            <div class="dn-empty__title">This dump has no heap statistics to show.</div>
                        }
                    </td>
                </tr>
            }
        </tbody>
    </table>
</div>
